          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Nicole:</strong> User Access control or UAC is a mandatory access control feature which was introduced by Microsoft to improve the security of Microsoft Windows by limiting user privileges until authorised by the administrator. This was done to prevent malware from compromising the operating system.</p>
<p>User access control generally manages user accounts particularly those with special access privileges to protect against misuse and unauthorised access.</p>
<p>To help keep a system secure, accounts should only be assigned to authorised individuals and should limit access to the minimum level needed by the individual.</p>
<p>Failure to implement an effective user access control management policy, may expose an organisations system to cyber attack as it makes it easier for hackers to take advantage of uncontrolled administrative privileges.</p>
<p>One of the techniques used to attack a system involves the elevation of privileges by guessing and cracking administrator passwords to gain access to target machines.</p>
<p>So, let’s look at how user access controls be managed effectively?</p>
<p>• By having a user account management system and privilege management process.</p>
<p>• Not using network and systems administrator accounts for other non-administrator activities.</p>
<p>• Restricting privileges to authorised individuals.</p>
<p>• Set up device-based authentication allowing only certain devices to access the network.</p>
<p>• Keeping records of user access permissions</p>
<p>• Implementing a strong password and unique username policy ensuring that passwords are changed regularly.</p>
<p>• Restricting the ability to install third party software</p>
<p>The overall goal of User Access Control is to protect the system and maintain the cyber security of an organisation or company. It is also used in households where parents can restrict internet access to children in the household but often for different reasons. It may annoy users that they may not have full access, but by limiting access to the absolute minimum needed to fulfil their role, there is less chance of cyber attacks happening therefore making the system more secure.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
